 @walleyeguy1  So an AMD processor that maxes out at a little over 4 ghz is only a little better than a Sandy Bridge CPU approaching 5 ghz? BETTER? At a much lower clock? I know Sandy is several generations old now, and Ivy, Haswell, Broadwell, and Skylake showed small IPC gains compared to Sandy over time, but by your own description it sounds like Zen has better IPC than Sandy and probably isn't far behind current Intel CPUs. The reason Intel performs better per core is because they run their cores at much higher clock speeds(like 5ghz+) at the cost of more power and heat.

Based on the most recent Steam hardware survey, 1080p represents over 60%(going on 61%) of displays in use. What amounts to 720p represents about another 20%. There are a few other resolutions represented between those as well.

Higher resolutions like 1440p, 4k and ultra wide resolutions each represent low single digits at best. Nvidia and Intel may have large margins on their high end silicon, but most people don't buy high end parts. They buy mid tier and lower. Most gamers don't demand all that much from their GPU based on those statistics. I would venture a guess that the vast majority of those 1080p and 720p panels are limited to 60hz refresh rate as well. Depending on the games they play, a mid range or lower GPU is plenty, and pretty much any Zen based CPU is more than fast enough to keep games at 60hz+. If a user turns their graphics options too high, the burden shifts to the GPU and it becomes the bottleneck, not the CPU. You don't need a high end CPU or GPU  to  have a good gaming experience. On the AMD side, Vega is the worst regarding power use but that can largely be mitigated with a bit of undervolting. Heat isn't an issue, and the few extra dollars per years spent on electricity make little difference in the grand scheme of things.

